3\n('Copa do Brasil', 'Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv') 1337\n('Serie A', 'BR-Football-Dataset.csv') 3689\n('Serie B', 'BR-Football-Dataset.csv') 3677\n('Serie C', 'BR-Football-Dataset.csv') 1807\nbrazilian players: 827\ntop clubs: [('Grêmio', 20), ('Atlético Mineiro', 20), ('Cruzeiro', 20), ('Fluminense', 20), ('Santos', 20), ('Internacional', 20), ('América FC (Minas Gerais)', 20), ('Botafogo', 20), ('Bahia', 20), ('Paraná', 20), ('Atlético Paranaense', 20), ('Vitória', 20), ('Sport Club do Recife', 20), ('Chapecoense', 20), ('Ceará Sporting Club', 20)]\ngabriels: ['Gabriel Jesus', 'Gabriel Paulista', 'Gabriel', 'Gabriel Boschilia', 'Gabrìel', 'Gabriel Silva', 'R. Pierre-Gabriel', 'Gabriel Xavier', 'Gabrielzinho', 'R. Gabrielsen']\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"venv/bin/python - <<'EOF'\nfrom brazilian_soccer import SoccerData\nd = SoccerData()\n# tournaments in BR dataset\nfrom collections import Counter\nc = Counter((m.competition, m.source_file) for m in d.matches)\nfor k,v in sorted(c.items()): print(k, v)\nbr = [p for p in d.players if p['Nationality']=='Brazil']\nclubs = Counter(p['Club'] for p in br if p['Club'])\nprint(\"brazilian players:\", len(br))\nprint(\"top clubs:\", clubs.most_common(15))\nnames = [p['Name'] for p in d.players if 'gabriel' in p['_norm_name']]\nprint(\"gabriels:\", names[:10])\nEOF","time":{"start":1788285628362,"end":1788285628407}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Findings:\n1. `team_stats` for \"Palmeiras\" season=2023 competition=\"Brasileirão\" → 0 matches. Why? Because competition filter: normalize_name(\"Brasileirão\") = \"brasileirao\". m.competition for the match file... wait, Brasileirão matches exist in seasons 2012-2022 only! Season 2023 doesn't exist in Brasileirao_Matches.csv. But Palmeiras played 2023 in BR-Football-Dataset (tournament names like \"Serie A\"?). Let me check tournaments in BR-Football-Dataset. Competition 2023 exists in that file. So \"Brasileirão\" filter excludes them. Fix: test with 2022, or make competition matching broader. Better: check what tournaments exist. The BR dataset has tournaments like \"Copa do Brasil\", \"Serie A\"? Let me check. Actually the cleanest: change test to 2022 which exists. But also standings issue:\n\n2. Standings 2019: 'Flamengo-RJ' 76 matches — that's 38 from Brasileirao_Matches + 38 more from novo_campeonato_brasileiro (2003-2019 includes 2019!). Both files cover overlapping seasons. So double-counting. Also 'Atletico-MG' 114 — includes BR-Football-Dataset \"Serie A\" 2019 (76 matches → 38+38? 114 = 38*3). Hmm, that means three files each have Serie A 2019: Brasileirao_Matches, novo, and BR-Football-Dataset (tournament name probably \"Serie A\" — normalize \"serie a\" — does \"brasileirao\" in \"serie a\"? No. But standings filter comp in normalize_name(m.competition) — \"brasileirao\" in \"serie a\"? No. So BR dataset not counted... but Atletico-MG 114 = 3×38.\n\nWait — maybe BR dataset tournament is named differently. 114 matches means 3 sources contributed. Also duplicate teams: 'Flamengo-RJ' vs 'Flamengo'? Team keys differ per file naming, but normalize_name strips -RJ so all flamengo variants unify. Flamengo 76 = 2 files. So BR dataset likely has tournament that contains \"brasileirao\"? Possibly \"Brasileirão\" tournament name in that file too. So three sources double/triple count.\n\nFix approach for standings: restrict standings to Brasileirão-specific matches only. But which file? Both Brasileirao_Matches (2012-2022) and novo (2003-2019) cover Brasileirão. For 2019, both cover it → double count even between those two.\n\nBetter approach: standings should use a single file per season. Priority: if season covered by Brasileirao_Matches (round-robin league file), use that; else use novo (2003-2011); else BR dataset. Implement standings with source selection: collect matches from files named in priority order, first file that has matches for that season wins.\n\nAlso team_stats and competition_stats have the same double-counting issue but tests only assert internal consistency; still, correctness matters — team_stats for a season sums across duplicate files. Let's apply the same \"one source per (competition, season)\" dedup in queries. Simpler: add a helper in SoccerData that returns matches for a competition+season picking a single best source file.\n\nLet me implement: in SoccerData, method `league_matches(competition, season)`: group matches matching competition by source_file; pick the file with most matches for that season (or priority list). Priority: Brasileirao_Matches.csv > novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv > BR-Football-Dataset.csv. Hmm but for tournaments like Copa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv and BR-Football-Dataset.csv both have it. Priority order file list first.\n\nActually simpler and robust: for standings use only matches where competition matches AND choose the source_file with the highest count for that season among matches matching competition; use only that file's matches. Let me implement `dedup_by_source(matches, season)`.\n\n3. Gabriel Barbosa not in FIFA dataset (it's FIFA ~2019 data; he might be at Inter Milan?). Players at Flamengo: 0 — FIFA dataset clubs don't include Flamengo. Adjust tests: search name \"Gabriel\" (Gabriel Jesus exists?). Let me check what Brazilian club names exist in fifa data. Clubs like \"Palmeiras\"? FIFA 19/20 doesn't have Brazilian league... Let me check.\n\n4. `barcelona-equ` regex fix: allow 2-3 letters suffix.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e2123a7001bP7kPrt7beYWGh","sessionID":"ses_fa1e38ef0ffeIEpkD87gUaZnL0","messageID":"msg_05e206a4900187msIeSUZbKRoU"}}
