lian_soccer/loader.py\n+++ /Users/maui/.retort/work/retort-local-tfe1ts5p/retort-af9123f1c3a8/brazilian_soccer/loader.py\n@@ -37,10 +37,10 @@\n s = unicodedata.normalize(\"NFKD\", name)\n s = \"\".join(c for c in s if not unicodedata.combining(c))\n # drop parenthetical annotations: \"Nacional (URU)\" -> \"Nacional\"\n s = re.sub(r\"\\([^)]*\\)\", \" \", s)\n-# drop state suffix: \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"Palmeiras\"\n-s = re.sub(r\"-\\s*[A-Z]{2}\\s*$\", \"\", s.strip())\n+# drop state suffix: \"Palmeiras-SP\" -> \"Palmeiras\", \"Barcelona-EQU\" -> \"Barcelona\"\n+s = re.sub(r\"-\\s*[A-Z]{2,3}\\s*$\", \"\", s.strip())\n s = re.sub(r\"\\s+\", \" \", s).strip().lower()\n return s\n \n \n","additions":2,"deletions":2},"truncated":false},"title":"brazilian_soccer/loader.py","time":{"start":1788285674479,"end":1788285674482}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Facts learned:\n- \"Serie A\" in BR dataset — this IS the Brasileirão semantically. And \"Brasileirão (2003-2019)\" is the historic file. For competition matching, \"Brasileirão\" query should also match \"Serie A\"? That's fuzzy. Keep competition filter as literal matching, but map: for standings/league queries, treat \"Serie A\" as Brasileirão. Also for team_stats the double-count issue: e.g., \"Palmeiras\" 2019 exists in Brasileirao_Matches AND novo AND BR dataset (Serie A 2019). team_stats sums all — wrong.\n\nFix strategy: dedupe by (source_file priority). Create in SoccerData a method `matches_for(competition=None, season=None)` that:\n1. Expands competition alias: \"brasileirao\" also matches \"Serie A\" and \"Brasileirão (2003-2019)\".\n2. Groups by source_file; picks ONE source file per (competition-group, season) using priority: dedicated cup files > BR dataset, newer dedicated > older. Simplest deterministic: priority list [Brasileirao_Matches, Brazilian_Cup_Matches, Libertadores_Matches, novo, BR-Football-Dataset] — pick the highest-priority file having ≥1 match for that season+competition.\n\nWait, but which seasons does each Brasileirão source cover? Brasileirao_Matches: 2012-2022. novo: 2003-2019. Serie A (BR dataset): which seasons? Let me not overthink: pick the file with the MOST matches for that competition+season (most complete), tie-break by priority. For 2019: Brasileirao_Matches 380, novo 380, Serie A ~? tie → priority picks Brasileirao_Matches. For 2003-2011: only novo. For 2023: only Serie A. \n\nCopa do Brasil: Brazilian_Cup (2012+) vs BR dataset (2023 era?). Most matches wins per season.\n\nAlso team name normalization: display 'Flamengo-RJ' etc — fine.\n\nAlso players: no Flamengo club in FIFA data (Brazilian clubs listed are different: Grêmio, Santos... but no Flamengo/Palmeiras/Corinthians/São Paulo — they're licensed elsewhere?). \"flamengo\" club count = 0 confirmed. So fix tests: use club \"Santos\" or \"Grêmio\" instead. \"Who plays for Flamengo\" is unanswerable — fine, adjust tests to real data. test \"forwards from São Paulo\" → use Santos. Gabriel Barbosa not present; test search by \"Gabriel Jesus\".\n\nCompetition aliasing: implement `COMP_ALIASES` mapping normalized names: \"brasileirao\" group includes \"serie a\", \"brasileirao (2003-2019)\". Implement helper `competition_matches(competition)` returning matches whose normalized competition is in the alias group.\n\nAlso team_stats with venue home for 2022 Corinthians — from Brasileirao_Matches only after dedup.\n\nRewrite: add to SoccerData:\n\n```python\n_SOURCE_PRIORITY = [\n    \"Brasileirao_Matches.csv\",\n    \"Brazilian_Cup_Matches.csv\",\n    \"Libertadores_Matches.csv\",\n    \"novo_campeonato_brasileiro.csv\",\n    \"BR-Football-Dataset.csv\",\n]\nCOMPETITION_ALIASES = {\n    \"brasileirao\": {\"brasileirao\", \"serie a\", \"brasileirao (2003-2019)\"},\n    \"copa do brasil\": {\"copa do brasil\"},\n    \"libertadores\": {\"copa libertadores\"},\n}\n\ndef competition_matches(self, competition=None, season=None):\n    if competition is None:\n        return list(self.matches)  # hmm dedup? leave all\n    key = normalize_name(competition)\n    group = None\n    for canon, aliases in COMPETITION_ALIASES.items():\n        if key in aliases:\n            group = aliases; break\n    if group is None:\n        # literal contains match\n        group = None\n    ...\n```\n\nSimpler: build alias-group resolution function `comp_group(name) -> set of normalized comp names`. If no alias group found, fall back to substring matches.\n\nThen `competition_matches(comp, season)`:\n- filter matches with m comp norm in group (or substring)\n- if season given, filter season\n- group by source_file; pick file with most matches (tie → priority index); return those matches.\n- if season is None: per-season dedup — do the selection per season across all seasons present.\n\nThen update queries: team_stats, find_matches (competition filter), standings, competition_stats, biggest_wins, best_team_record to use `data.competition_matches(...)` where a competition is specified. For find_matches with competition=None, keep all (duplicates across files exist but that's \"dataset coverage\"; fine).\n\nActually find_matches with competition specified should also dedup. Use competition_matches then apply other filters.\n\nAlso normalize regex fix for -EQU: `-\\s*[A-Z]{2,3}\\s*$`. But careful: \"Sport-PE\" fine. But real team \"Bahia-B\" ? unlikely. Use {2,3}.\n\nBut wait: display names like \"Atlético-MG\" — state suffix. Some Libertadores names \"Barcelona-EQU\" 3 letters → good. But \"Gremio-RS\"? fine. Edge: team names ending like \"Vila Nova-GO\" ok. Risk: \"Atletico-MG\" vs some team actually hyphenated name... acceptable.\n\nUpdate queries.py accordingly, and fix tests:\n- test_team_statistics: use season 2022 (in Brasileirao_Matches).\n- test_search_player_by_name: \"Gabriel Jesus\".\n- test_players_at_club: use \"Santos\".\n- test_forwards_from_sao_paulo → club \"Santos\", position \"ST\". Check Santos has ST players? Santos 20 players surely has ST.\n- standings 2019: after dedup, Flamengo 38 matches, champion. \n- test_standings_points_math uses standings 2019 fine.\n\nAlso update test \"test_matches_have_required_fields\"? m.competition and home/away — ok.\n\nAlso team_stats for 2023 Palmeiras + \"Brasileirão\": now with aliasing \"Serie A\" 2023 exists in BR dataset → Palmeiras 2023 matches found. Good, test can stay at 2023? test asserted wins+draws+losses==matches; fine either way. Keep 2023.\n\nLet me now edit loader.py.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05e21df35001OV578D2tmP0rUW","sessionID":"ses_fa1e38ef0ffeIEpkD87gUaZnL0","messageID":"msg_05e213234001OdEmAsMl9O7mM7"}}
